    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old patient is undergoing a total joint arthroplasty. The surgical team is reviewing the risk of perioperative systemic complications. Regarding urinary tract infection, which of the following is most accurate in this setting?', '["It is completely preventable with prophylactic antibiotics", "It is a rare complication seen almost exclusively in young patients", "It only occurs after the 3-month postoperative mark", "Associated with prolonged indwelling catheterization", "It is solely caused by the type of implant bearing surface used"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Urinary tract infection is a known systemic complication following major joint arthroplasty. Specifically, it is associated with prolonged indwelling catheterization. Proper medical optimization and perioperative care are crucial for risk mitigation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old patient is 8 weeks out from a primary total knee arthroplasty and complains of severe stiffness. Range of motion is 10 to 70 degrees. After failing aggressive physical therapy, what is the next most appropriate step in management?', '["Open arthrolysis and synovectomy", "Arthroscopic lysis of adhesions", "Revision total knee arthroplasty", "Manipulation under anesthesia (MUA)", "Long-term casting in extension"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Stiffness or arthrofibrosis after TKA is a frustrating complication. If aggressive physical therapy fails, the most appropriate next step within the first 2-3 months is typically a manipulation under anesthesia (MUA) to break up intra-articular adhesions and improve range of motion.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old female undergoes a total hip arthroplasty via a standard anterolateral approach. Postoperatively, the patient is at highest risk for dislocation if the hip is placed in which of the following positions?', '["Flexion, abduction, and external rotation", "Extension, abduction, and internal rotation", "Flexion, adduction, and external rotation", "Extension and external rotation", "Extension and internal rotation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a anterolateral approach to the hip, the typical position of instability causing dislocation is extension and external rotation. The Superior gluteal nerve is also at risk during this approach. Patients are routinely placed on specific hip precautions to avoid these extreme ranges of motion early in the postoperative period.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old patient presents for evaluation 8 years after a total hip arthroplasty of the left side. They report new-onset mechanical symptoms. Radiographs are unremarkable. What is a key initial step in evaluating for an aseptic versus septic etiology?', '["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and C-reactive protein (CRP)", "Immediate revision surgery", "Bone scan", "MRI with metal artifact reduction", "Diagnostic arthroscopy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In evaluating a painful total joint arthroplasty, distinguishing between aseptic loosening and infection is critical. Initial laboratory screening should include ESR and CRP. If these are elevated, an aspiration is usually indicated.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old patient presents for evaluation 9 years after a total shoulder arthroplasty of the right side. They report new-onset mechanical symptoms. Radiographs are unremarkable. What is a key initial step in evaluating for an aseptic versus septic etiology?', '["Bone scan", "Diagnostic arthroscopy", "Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and C-reactive protein (CRP)", "MRI with metal artifact reduction", "Immediate revision surgery"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In evaluating a painful total joint arthroplasty, distinguishing between aseptic loosening and infection is critical. Initial laboratory screening should include ESR and CRP. If these are elevated, an aspiration is usually indicated.'),
