    (v_topic_id, 'A 51-year-old patient presents for evaluation 10 years after a total shoulder arthroplasty of the left side. They report new-onset mechanical symptoms. Radiographs are unremarkable. What is a key initial step in evaluating for an aseptic versus septic etiology?', '["Diagnostic arthroscopy", "MRI with metal artifact reduction", "Bone scan", "Immediate revision surgery", "Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and C-reactive protein (CRP)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In evaluating a painful total joint arthroplasty, distinguishing between aseptic loosening and infection is critical. Initial laboratory screening should include ESR and CRP. If these are elevated, an aspiration is usually indicated.'),
